name damage-amraam-1
timeout 700

# THE HIT, AND WHAT COMES AFTER IT. bvr-intercept.fbm proves the shot; this mission exists to show the
# CONSEQUENCE, which that one cannot: there the target carries objectives, so the moment it is shot down
# its own mission monitor concludes FAIL and the run stops one tick after the burst. Here the target has
# NO waypoints at all — a unit a mission gives no objectives carries no FBMissionMonitor
# (app/FBMissionBoot.h) and therefore cannot end the run — so the simulation keeps running through the
# whole aftermath and only stops when the physics judge (core/FBFlightMonitor) says the wreck hit the
# ground. Exit 2 (CRASH) is the intended outcome, and it is the honest one: nothing kills this jet, its
# own damage does.
#
# THE TARGET IS THE PRIMARY ACTOR, deliberately: index 0 owns telemetry.csv and, in the native oracle,
# the camera and the HUD. So `gpu_native --mission missions/damage-amraam.fbm --interval N` renders the
# cockpit of the aircraft being shot at — which is where the consequence chain is visible as pixels
# (dashed readouts on every source whose box is gone).
#
# WHAT THE RUN SHOWS, in order, all in events.log + telemetry.csv:
#   stores DETONATION    the burst: miss distance, closure, time of flight (measured on the poses)
#   damage DAMAGE        the resolution: which zone, at what range, at what fragment energy
#   damage SYSTEM        one line per system that changed state
#   damage KILL          the moment the airframe stopped being able to fly its sortie
#   dmg_failed/dmg_degraded  the same picture as a time series, from the tick of the burst onwards
#   bus_*                every block of a failed box turning Invalid (core/FBStateBusTelemetry)
#   the FDM columns      what a jet with no thrust and no control authority does next
#
# THE SHOOTER is bvr-intercept's viper, unchanged: the same brief (a vector and a master-arm call), the
# same CRM search, the same two AIM-120s, the same intercept task. Nothing about the shot is scripted.

# THE GEOMETRY is bvr-intercept's, turned 90 degrees onto a north/south line: 100 km apart, head-on,
# co-altitude at 8,000 m, closing at ~680 m/s. It runs north/south rather than east/west for one reason,
# and it is a property of the pilot rather than of this mission: a unit with NO flight plan has no
# briefed vector, so its intercept pilot holds the heading its own state carried at its first decision
# tick — which is 000, because that tick happens before the first FDM step has written an attitude. So
# the target is spawned on 000 and its declared heading, its initial condition and the heading it then
# flies are all the same number. (That the anchor is 000 rather than the spawn heading is a wart worth
# fixing in the pilot; this mission works with it instead of hiding it.)

unit target
  module f16
  team hostile
  spawn 46.40000 7.70000 8000 0.0 400
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off                         # it never looks and never warns anyone
  set rwr off
  set task intercept                       # no waypoints: nothing can end the run on its behalf

unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 47.30000 7.70000 8000 180.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 45.00000 7.70000 8000 450             # the vector; 150 km south, so it is never reached
